@@s001 Counting the steps: big-O
@@s002 For n = 8 I compare four functions. The bars are on a <b>logarithmic scale</b>: each notch more multiplies by 10, it does not add.
@@s003 <b>log n = 3</b> and <b>n = 8</b>: they grow slowly.
@@s004 <b>n log n = 24</b>: the mergesort. A little more than n, but already above.
@@s005 <b>n² = 64</b>: the selection sort, the pairs. <b>2ⁿ = 256</b>: the brute force on the subsets. For n = 30 the latter is already 10⁹.
@@s006 dato:logarithm,due:n log n,ora:quadratic,no:exponential
@@s007 Counting the steps: big-O
@@s008 An algorithm is compared with another by counting how many steps it takes, as a function of the size of the input. The big-O notation says which term dominates when the size grows.
@@s009 The size of the input
@@s010 Time is not measured in seconds: it is measured in steps, as a function of a <b>size</b> of the input. The size is the number of elements: <code>n</code> nodes, <code>m</code> edges, <code>W</code> budget. In exam texts the time must be written with the <b>names of the parameters of the text</b>, not with a generic <code>n</code>.
@@s011 Big-O: the term that dominates
@@s012 <code>f(n) = O(g(n))</code> means: from a certain point on, <code>f(n)</code> is at most a constant times <code>g(n)</code>. Only the dominant term is kept, and no constant: <code>3n² + 5n + 7 = O(n²)</code>.
@@s013 <code>O(log n)</code> binary search;
@@s014 <code>O(n)</code> a scan; <code>O(n + m)</code> a visit of a graph;
@@s015 <code>O(n log n)</code> sorting by comparisons;
@@s016 <code>O(n²)</code> double nested loop; <code>O(2ⁿ)</code> subsets.
@@s017 Four functions compared for n = 8
@@s018 How to count
@@s019 Loops in sequence are <b>added</b>, nested loops are <b>multiplied</b>. Count how many times the innermost line runs, not how many lines there are. A recursive function becomes a recurrence: <code>T(n) = T(n−1) + O(1)</code> gives <code>O(n)</code>, <code>T(n) = T(n−1) + T(n−2)</code> gives the growth of Fibonacci, about 1.618ⁿ.
@@s020 Bounds: O, Ω, Θ
@@s021 <code>O</code> is an upper bound. <code>Ω</code> (omega) is a lower bound. <code>Θ</code> (theta) is the case where they coincide: the cost is exactly that. Mergesort is <code>Θ(n log n)</code>; sorting by comparisons is <code>Ω(n log n)</code>, so mergesort is optimal among the comparison sorts.
@@s022 At the exam
@@s023 At the exam
@@s024 Write the time with the parameters of the text: "O(n + m)", "O(nW)", "O(N²)" with N = levels. Do not write "O(n)" if the text has no n. When the question says <i>"don't solve the recurrence"</i>, stop the answer at the recurrence.
